# Documentation updates

When a change affects behavior, APIs, configuration, CLI, or user-facing workflows that are already documented, update the matching documentation in the same change.

## When docs must change

- Public API or request/response shape changes
- New, removed, or renamed parameters, options, env vars, or defaults
- Behavior changes that would make an existing tutorial, guide, or example wrong
- CLI flags, commands, or output that docs describe
- Error messages, status codes, or auth flows covered in docs

```text
❌ BAD: ship a param rename and leave tutorials/examples on the old name
✅ GOOD: update code, tests, and every docs/example reference that would mislead a reader
```

## How to update

- Prefer the repository’s existing docs layout and conventions (`docs/`, `docs_src/`, README generation scripts, release notes, etc.).
- Update the nearest authoritative page and any executable examples that would fail or mislead after the change.
- Keep docs changes scoped to the impacted behavior; do not rewrite unrelated pages.
- If docs intentionally stay unchanged (deprecated path still documented, internal-only change), say so in the handoff.

## Before finishing

- Search for stale names/snippets tied to the change.
- Run any docs-related checks the repo already provides when they apply.
- Mention doc updates (or the explicit no-docs rationale) in the Developer/QA/PM handoff.
